{"name":"Melodic White","hex":"#eee8e8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melodic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or having melody.","Melodious, tuneful."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melodic"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melody","blocks":[{"text":"A melody, also tune, voice, or line, is a linear succession of musical tones that the listener perceives as a single entity. In its most literal sense, a melody is a combination of pitch and rhythm, while more figuratively, the term can include other musical elements such as tonal color. It is the foreground to the background accompaniment. A line or part need not be a foreground melody."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/BachFugueBar.png/330px-BachFugueBar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melody"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Pop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG/500px-Pop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Pop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG/1280px-Pop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Pop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG/500px-Pop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Pop Goes the Weasel\" melody ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APop_Goes_the_Weasel_melody.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f0/Webern_Variations_melody.png/500px-Webern_Variations_melody.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f0/Webern_Variations_melody.png/500px-Webern_Variations_melody.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Melody from Anton Webern's Variations for orchestra, Op. 30 (pp. 23–24)[6] ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWebern_Variations_melody.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/500px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_John_Paul_II_%2814037015405%29.jpg/1280px-Canonization_2014-The_Canonization_of_Saint_John_XXIII_and_Saint_J